{"reference":"Job 28","book":"Job","chapter":28,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Who Speaks in This Poem?","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The Hebrew text does not identify this poem’s speaker. It may continue Job’s speech, function as a narrator’s interlude, or stand as a distinct wisdom poem placed here by the book’s composer. The conclusion about fearing the Lord and turning from evil is clear, but assigning the poem to a particular character or treating it as the final resolution of the dispute remains an interpretive decision.","verse_refs":["Job 28:1-28"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_028_b65b3377ba81b2776e124e3c"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Poem Within Job’s Argument","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"Job 28 contrasts human success in bringing hidden ore to light with humanity’s inability to discover where wisdom is found. In the following chapters, Job’s case reaches its climax through memories of his former life, ethical self-testimony, and a formal demand that the Almighty answer. The relationship between this poem and those chapters is debated, but the sequence places the question of wisdom alongside Job’s continuing search for an answer.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_028_7dab846d71bdfe1b06670ae2"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"A later passage names Job among examples associated with suffering, patience, endurance, and the Lord’s compassionate outcome. In that broader reading, Job’s steadfastness can mean persistence through his story or its eventual outcome; it should not be taken to require silent endurance of abuse or to forbid seeking safety and protection.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_028_d7dd579ff75cea1c91d52e54"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}